G1="Unity without truth"
G2="Unity without justice"
G3="Unity without mercy"
G4="Unity without sacrifice"
G5="Father and mother: justice and mercy held together"
E=[
(G1,294,"Consider the family that keeps the peace by never naming the father's drinking, the nation \"unified\" by propaganda, the parish that papers over a grave error to avoid an awkward conversation.","Consider the family that keeps the peace by never naming the father's drinking, the husband and wife who stopped arguing only because they stopped telling each other what they really think, the parish that papers over a grave error to avoid an awkward conversation."),
(G1,294,"Yet notice that truth cannot stand alone either: truth without mercy hardens into cold, accusing orthodoxy that drives wounded people out the door.","Yet notice that truth cannot stand alone either. Truth without mercy hardens into cold accusation: the parent who is always right and never kind, until the children stop confiding at all; the parish whose orthodoxy drives wounded people out the door."),
(G2,296,"what looks like communion is really some people using others: the \"unity\" of a cartel, of insiders who cover for one another, of a marriage in which one spouse's needs are always sacrificed to the other's comfort.","what looks like communion is really some people using others: the mother who cooks, cleans, drives and remembers every birthday while the family treats it as simply her job; the one sibling who cares for an aging parent alone while the others come for holidays; the child whose gifts are barely noticed beside a favored brother's; the marriage in which one spouse's needs are always sacrificed to the other's comfort."),
(G2,296,"pressed without mercy, it curdles into relentless score-keeping that no real relationship survives.","pressed without mercy, it curdles into relentless score-keeping that no real relationship survives. Think of the controlling, over-critical father whose rules are never wrong and whose approval is never given: his children obey while they must, and leave as soon as they can."),
(G3,298,"The family that will not forgive hardens into estrangement; the community that cannot absolve","The family that will not forgive hardens into estrangement: the brothers who have not spoken since their father's funeral, the daughter still punished for a mistake she made at sixteen. The community that cannot absolve"),
(G3,298,"But mercy untethered from truth and justice degenerates into mere enabling—a false peace","But mercy untethered from truth and justice degenerates into mere enabling: the mother who pays her grown son's debts again and again, covers for him with his father, and never lets him face what he has done. That is a false peace"),
(G4,300,"Friendships that evaporate when the price rises, marriages that dissolve the moment \"my needs aren't being met,\" congregations that scatter under the first real pressure","Friendships that evaporate when the price rises, marriages that dissolve the moment \"my needs aren't being met,\" parents who give their children everything except their time, congregations that scatter under the first real pressure"),
(G4,300,"or extracted from the weak by the strong, which is not love at all.","or extracted from the weak by the strong, which is not love at all. The mother who gives and gives while no one in the house is ever asked to give anything back is not building communion; she is wearing herself out, and the family learns to take.\n\nMany families know the pattern behind these examples: a father who leans toward justice, a mother who leans toward mercy. This is part of how God made us. The Church teaches that the \"difference and complementarity\" of man and woman \"are oriented toward the goods of marriage and the flourishing of family life\" (CCC 2333). Each needs the other. His justice without her mercy crushes a child; her mercy without his justice spoils one. Held together, they show a child what God's love looks like, for in our Father in heaven, justice and mercy are one."),
]
